\begin_layout Subsection
Conventions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Time.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We omit time indices except where an equation contains a lead or a lag. Where time appears it is a subscript on the variable, as in 
\begin_inset Formula $W_{t}$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{t+1}$
\end_inset

. In the source code every variable carries an explicit time index.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Reuse of names across levels of aggregation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The same letter denotes the same economic object at different levels of detail, distinguished only by how many indices it carries. 
\begin_inset Formula $Y$
\end_inset

 is total output, 
\begin_inset Formula $Y_{p}$
\end_inset

 is output of product 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $Y_{p,i}$
\end_inset

 is output of product 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 by industry 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

. We introduce a new letter only for a genuinely new object, not for a finer cut of an existing one.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Sums versus quantity aggregates.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A variable with fewer indices is by default the simple sum of the same variable with one more index, 
\begin_inset Formula \[
Y=\sum_{i}Y_i
\]
\end_inset

and we omit such accounting identities for brevity. The exception matters: where a CES or CET equation defines a quantity aggregate, the aggregate is 
\shape italic
not
\shape default
 the sum of its components. In the production nest, 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{N}$
\end_inset

 is a CES aggregate of the 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{f}$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $f\in N$
\end_inset

, and in the CET block 
\begin_inset Formula $Y_{i}$
\end_inset

 is a CET aggregate of the 
\begin_inset Formula $Y_{p,i}$
\end_inset

. Adding the components of either will not reproduce the aggregate away from the base year. Where it is not obvious from context which of the two is meant, the surrounding text says so.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Prices and values.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Aggregate prices are defined implicitly by the value identity 
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\cdot X=\sum_{x}P_{x}X_{x}
\]
\end_inset

so an aggregate price is an implicit deflator rather than an independent variable, and value additivity holds by construction even where quantity additivity does not. Base-year prices are set to one, so in the calibration year measured volumes equal values.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Variables and parameters.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Latin letters denote variables. Greek letters denote parameters calibrated in the base year – share parameters 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset

, elasticities 
\begin_inset Formula $E$
\end_inset

, markups 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

, unit-content coefficients 
\begin_inset Formula $\kappa$
\end_inset

. Greek does not mean permanently fixed: as Section
\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:layers"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 sets out, a satellite module extends the core precisely by endogenising one of these parameters. A Greek letter is therefore best read at 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

constant in the core, candidate for endogenisation in an extension
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Superscripts and subscripts.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Subscripts are indices. Superscripts are qualifiers naming a variant of the same object: 
\begin_inset Formula $P^{Y}$
\end_inset

 is a basic price, 
\begin_inset Formula $P^{D}$
\end_inset

 a purchaser price, 
\begin_inset Formula $P^{I}$
\end_inset

 an investment price, 
\begin_inset Formula $Q^{S}$
\end_inset

 physical supply against 
\begin_inset Formula $Q^{D}$
\end_inset

 physical demand and 
\begin_inset Formula $Q^{Y}$
\end_inset

 physical output, 
\begin_inset Formula $C^{\mathrm{HtM}}$
\end_inset

 the hand-to-mouth part of consumption. A superscript is never an index: 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu^{S}_{s,u}$
\end_inset

 is a margin-service share carrying the indices 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

, not a share carrying a further index 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Hats.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A hat distinguishes a fixed weight from a realised share where the same letter denotes both. In the import-substitution equation, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\mu}_{p,u,o}$
\end_inset

 is the calibrated weight held fixed, while 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{p,u,o}$
\end_inset

 is the origin share that the resulting allocation implies. Where only one of the two is ever needed – as in the production and consumption nests – the weight is written unhatted.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Hooks.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Core equations include friction terms that are identically zero unless a satellite module supplies an equation for them. They are written out in full rather than folded into the surrounding expression, so that a satellite can be attached without rewriting the core equation.
\end_layout

