\begin_layout Subsection
How satellite modules attach
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:attachment"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A satellite module does not define variables that other modules read. Where two extensions need to communicate, they do so through a core module acting as intermediary, so that the number of direct linkages between modules stays small. The practical form of this rule: a labour-supply extension should not read a specific policy variable out of a tax extension. Instead the core carries a marginal labour income tax rate, the tax extension determines it, and the labour extension reads it – neither needs to know anything about the other.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Extensions attach in two ways.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Endogenising a core parameter.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The core writes a calibrated share and treats it as constant; a satellite supplies an equation that determines it instead. The accounting core allocates demand across origins with fixed shares, 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{p,u,o}=\mu_{p,u,o}D_{p,u}$
\end_inset

. the import-substitution module replaces that closure with a price-responsive equation for the same 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{p,u,o}$
\end_inset

. No core equation is rewritten; a parameter simply becomes a variable.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Filling a friction hook.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The core writes a term that is identically zero unless something supplies it. The user-cost of capital contains a marginal capital adjustment cost, and output at the top nest is net of friction losses. With no adjustment-cost satellite attached, both terms are zero and the expressions collapse to their frictionless forms. Writing the hook out explicitly, rather than folding it into the surrounding algebra, is what makes the satellite attachable without touching the core.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Sector accounts
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:sector-accounts"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Sector accounting keeps track of the financial assets and liabilities of all sectors, and of all transfers between them. It belongs to the accounting core: the identities it enforces are ones any internally consistent dataset my satisfy, and the portfolio splits it needs beyond them are fixed base-year shares – default closures in the sense of §
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:layers"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

, which a satellite can replace.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Our sectors match the national accounts, except that we lump non-profit institutions serving households together with households – the same treatment the input-output system gives the household consumption column.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Sectors are not industries. An industry (Danish 
\shape italic
branche
\shape default
) groups establishments by what they produce and is classified by NACE; a sector groups institutional units by what they are – who owns them and how they are financed – and is classified by ESA. The two cut across each other: non-financial corporations operate in nearly every industry, and the education industry contains government units, corporations and non-profits alike. In this document, industries carry production and are indexed by 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

; sectors carry financial positions and are indexed by 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
\text{sectors}=\left\{ \text{Households},\text{Government},
\text{Financial corporations},\text{Non-financial corporations},
\text{Rest of the world}\right\}
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
What the accounts do and do not contain
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The accounts hold financial positions only. Non-financial assets are excluded: the capital stock lives in the production block, and the two are never consolidated into a single balance sheet. A sector's net worth in the ordinary sense cannot be read off this system.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
That exclusion is exactly what makes the system close. Every financial asset is some other sector's liability, whereas a building is nobody's liability. Once the rest of the world is included as a sector, every net variable therefore sums to zero across sectors – net assets, financial income, financial transfers, and revaluations alike.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Accounting identities
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $W_{s}$
\end_inset

 be net financial assets of sector 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

, an end-of-period stock; let 
\begin_inset Formula $A^{\bullet}_{s}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $L^{\bullet}_{s}$
\end_inset

 be gross assets and gross liabilities in instrument 
\begin_inset Formula $\bullet$
\end_inset

; let 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{NL}_{s}$
\end_inset

 be net financial transactions (net lending or borrowing), and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{REV}_{s}$
\end_inset

 revaluations.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The condensed budget constraint states that the change in net assets is what the sector lent plus what its existing holdings were revalued by: 
\begin_inset Formula \[
W_{s,t}-W_{s,t-1}=\mathrm{NL}_{s,t}+\mathrm{REV}_{s,t}
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Net positions decompose by instrument into debt and equity: 
\begin_inset Formula \[
W_{s}=\left(A^{\mathrm{debt}}_{s}-L^{\mathrm{debt}}_{s}\right)
    +\left(A^{\mathrm{eq}}_{s}-L^{\mathrm{eq}}_{s}\right)
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Every net variable sums to zero over sectors, 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\sum_{s}W_{s}=0,\qquad
\sum_{s}\mathrm{NL}_{s}=0,\qquad
\sum_{s}\mathrm{REV}_{s}=0
\]
\end_inset

and the same holds instrument by instrument, for example 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\sum_{s}\left(A^{\mathrm{eq}}_{s}-L^{\mathrm{eq}}_{s}\right)=0
\]
\end_inset

Financial income closes for the same reason: interest received by one sector is interest paid by another, so it nets to zero economy-wide.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Together these are the accounting core's test. A dataset that violates them leaves a residual, and the variable, sector and period on which it sits identify the inconsistency. Because the default closure makes the rest of the world the residual holder, the core has to report the imbalance before closing it – otherwise an inconsistent dataset solves and the error is hidden in the rest-of-the-world account.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Sector modules vs sector accounts
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Each sector corresponds to one or more agents, and each has a dedicated module. Those modules contain the sector-specific specification of net financial transactions – net income and expenditure in the sector's budget constraint. The sector accounts then reconcile those specifications against the condensed budget constraint above, which is deliberately the same identity for every sector.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The correspondence is not one-to-one, because one module covers both corporate sectors.
\end_layout

