\begin_layout Standard
The household case is worth spelling out, because the same object appears twice under different names. Household wealth 
\begin_inset Formula $W$
\end_inset

 in the consumption-savings problem is the household row of 
\begin_inset Formula $W_{s}$
\end_inset

 here. There are not two accounting systems that must be made to agree – there is one budget constraint, written at two levels of detail, which is what 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

reconciled
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 means above.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The return 
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset

 in that constraint is interest and dividends per unit of wealth carried in from the previous period. Revaluations are not folded into it: they enter the budget constraint as a separate term, and the expected return the household optimises against excludes them. Capital gains are therefore unanticipated – realised wealth changes by more than the household planned for.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Revaluations are exogenous by default. Each sector carries a revaluation rate calibrated in the base year and applied to its net financial position, and the sector accounts take the resulting revaluation as given. Making the equity revaluation rate endogenous – determined by a discounted cash-flow valuation of the firm, so that the sector accounts consume it rather than set it – is specified in §
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:firms"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

. The current implementation does not do it: revaluation rates are exogenous. It has one constraint to respect: revaluations must net to zero across sectors. A single rate applied to equity positions delivers that automatically, because equity nets to zero; sector-specific rates applied to net financial assets do not.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Splitting net positions into assets and liabilities
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Each sector module must specify how net saving is split between assets and liabilities, and between instruments. By default these portfolio shares are calibrated in the base year and are held fixed – a default closure in the sense of §
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:layers"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

, which a satellite could endogenise.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Carrying gross assets and liabilities rather than net positions alone is a practical choice. The alternative is to introduce an extra asset type purely to distinguish domestic from foreign equity. With explicit liabilities that distinction comes for free: the equity liability of the rest of the world is, by construction, the total foreign equity owned by domestic sectors 
\begin_inset Formula \[
L^{\mathrm{eq}}_{\mathrm{RoW}}=\text{foreign equity held by resident sectors}
\]
\end_inset

and no new asset dimension is needed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Input output
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:io"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The input output system keeps track of all flows of goods and services. It belongs to the accounting core: it is calibrated directly to published tables and, as far as possible, contains no assumptions about economic behaviour. Its fixed shares are default closures that behavioural modules replace.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Where a behavioural module needs more detail than the published tables carry, that detail enters as a 
\shape italic
satellite sub-table
\shape default
 which must aggregate back to the published cells – never as extra columns on the main table. The core can therefore always be run on the published table alone, and each extra split can be swtiched off independently.
\end_layout

