\begin_layout Standard
Read a column: manufacturing bought 80 of electricity and 40 of refined fuels. That is 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{p,t}$
\end_inset

 for those products. Or, to be more specific, 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{\text{electricity},\text{mfg}}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{\text{refined fuels},\text{mfg}}$
\end_inset

. Read the investment column: 120 of construction output was sold as capital formation, which is 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{\text{construction},K}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
What the table does not say is equally important. It does not say whether manufacturing's electricity heated buildings or ran process equipment. It does not say how much of the household's 50 of electricity was space heating and how much charged a car. And it does not say which industry bought the 120 of construction, or whether that investment was a building or a vehicle. Those three questions are the subject of Section
\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:satellites"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Our default assumption for the product mix inside a use is fixed shares: 
\begin_inset Formula \[
D_{p,u}=\mu_{p,u}D_u
\]
\end_inset

with 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{p,u}$
\end_inset

 read off the published table. Other modules can endogenise these shares to allow substitution. Private consumption is the exception in one direction – the consumption-split of Section
\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:cons-types"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 replaces this use-level mapping – and inventories are the exception in the other, as set out below.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Industry split (supply table)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The same product can be produced by more than one industry. Our default assumption is fixed industry shares for each product.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
Y_{p,i}=\mu_{p,i}Y_{p}
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Endogenous industry shares
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
CET split
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:cet"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When industry shares are endogenous, perfect transformation determines 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{p,i}$
\end_inset

 and sets 
\begin_inset Formula $MC_{p,i}=MC_{i}$
\end_inset

. For selected cells, CET replaces this closure with
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
Y_{p,i}=\hat{\mu}^{CET}_{p,i}\left(\frac{MC_{p,i}}{MC_{i}}\right)^{E^{CET}_{i}}Y_{i}
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $Y_{i}$
\end_inset

 is the CET aggregate. CET keeps 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\mu}^{CET}_{p,i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $E^{CET}_{i}$
\end_inset

 fixed and determines 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{p,i}$
\end_inset

 through the output allocation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula $MC_{i}$
\end_inset

 is marginal cost before the product split. 
\begin_inset Formula $MC_{p,i}$
\end_inset

 includes product-specific production taxes or subsidies:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
MC_{p,i}=MC^{\text{resource}}_{p,i}+\frac{\partial T^{\text{production}}_{i}}{\partial Y_{p,i}}
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
User taxes, VAT, purchaser-price product taxes, and trade margins do not enter 
\begin_inset Formula $MC_{p,i}$
\end_inset

; they enter the purchaser price. By construction,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
\sum_{p}MC_{p,i}Y_{p,i}=MC_{i}Y_{i}
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Pricing uses 
\begin_inset Formula $MC_{p,i}$
\end_inset

; CET adds no markup.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Country of origin
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:origin"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We split all direct product demand by country of origin, 
\begin_inset Formula \[
D_{p,u,o}=\mu_{p,u,o}D_{p,u},\quad o=\left\{\text{domestic},\text{import}\right\}
\]
\end_inset

Using an index for origin rather than a separate import tables makes it easy to split imports later, for example into euro-area and rest-of-the-world.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Inventories
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Inventory demand by product and origin, 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{p,\mathrm{INV},o}$
\end_inset

, may be positive or negative. Its level is a fixed share of the output of the industry holding it: the inventory-to-output ratio is calibrated in the base year and held fixed thereafter, so inventories move with output. The product and origin shares above apply to it as to any other use.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Import substitution
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:import-sub"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To introduce import substitution with a satellite model, endogenise 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{p,u,o}$
\end_inset

. Substitution by product, rather than also by use, may well be enough. Demand responds to relative purchaser prices: 
\begin_inset Formula \[
D_{p,u,o}=\hat{\mu}_{p,u,o}\left(\frac{P^{D}_{p,u,o}}{P^{D}_{p,u}}\right)^{-E_{p}}D_{p,u}
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Tourism
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Tourism needs a special entry because the household and trade accounts classify the same purchase in different ways. The household budget uses the resident concept: 
\begin_inset Formula $C$
\end_inset

 covers purchases by resident households at home and abroad. National accounts count purchases in the country by non-resident household as exports.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Eurostat reports the household product cells under the domestic concept – purchases in the country by residents and non-residents alike – and gives only total adjustments for residents' purchases abroad (
\family typewriter
OP_RES
\family default
) and non-residents' purchases in the country (
\family typewriter
OP_NRES
\family default
). Neither adjustment is published by product, so tourist demand cannot be given its own product mix from published data.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Current treatment.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The two are not separated. Residents' purchases abroad are carried as a consumption type of their own; non-residents' purchases are added into the household cells, and the input-output private-consumption aggregate is their sum, 
\begin_inset Formula \[
D_{C}=C+C^{\mathrm{NonRes}}
\]
\end_inset

The household budget uses 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{C}$
\end_inset

 rather than 
\begin_inset Formula $C$
\end_inset

, so resident consumption currently includes non-residents' purchases in the country.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Proposed treatment.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Carry 
\begin_inset Formula $C^{\mathrm{NonRes}}$
\end_inset

 as its own column. The household budget then contains only 
\begin_inset Formula $C$
\end_inset

, the rest-of-the-world account picks up 
\begin_inset Formula $C^{\mathrm{NonRes}}$
\end_inset

 in exports, and tourist demand enters the product balance once, through 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{C}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note that the two trourism items are opposite flows: the tourism consumption type of §
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:cons-types"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 is resident's spending abroad, an import, while 
\begin_inset Formula $C^{\mathrm{NonRes}}$
\end_inset

 is non-residents' spending here, an export.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Satellite sub-tables
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:satellites"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Three separate questions ask for detail the published use table does not carry. All three are handled the same way, and it is worth stating the pattern once before applying it three times.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In each case we know a 
\shape italic
margin
\shape default
 – a published row or column total – and we do not know the 
\shape italic
interior
\shape default
 of the sub-table. The interior is filled from a prior (Danish accounts, JRC-IDEES process shares, or a pooled donor country) and then scaled so that it reproduces the published margin exactly. Nothing in the accounting core changes: turn the sub-table off and the core still balances against the published cells.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The three applications are collected in Table
\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:three-splits"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

. They share an algorithm, but they concern entirely different cells.
\end_layout

