\begin_layout Standard
The factory needed 40 of heat, produced half from a gas boiler and half from a heat pump; 60 of process energy, all electrici; and 20 of transport, all diesel. Adding across the bundles returns the two published cells. Recipe shares sum to one down each column, so with base-year prices of one a bundle's quantity is simply what was spent on it.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This is a sub-table, not a set of new input-output columns. With the sub-table off, each industry has a single intermediate bundle. With it on, purposes substitute against each other when relative prices move.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
FIGARO and the national supply-use tables give the row totals. They do not give GREU's purposes. Eurostat's physical energy flow accounts (
\family typewriter
eng_ac_pefasu
\family default
) are industry 
\begin_inset Formula $\times$
\end_inset

 energy product in physical units; they do not distinguish heating from process use, and they do not identify own-account transport by the industry that operates it. The practical route is to take process shares from JRC-IDEES, or Danish energy accounts, as a prior and scale to the published 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{p,i}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Why the shares carry an industry index.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Dropping the 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 from 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{p,m,i}$
\end_inset

 – giving every industry the same recipe for a given purpose – is neither needed nor helpful. Note that this is a different assumption from the fixed shares used elsewhere: fixed means constant over time, with each industry keeping its own. A common recipe makes every industry's intermediate column a mixture of the same 
\begin_inset Formula $|m|$
\end_inset

 recipe vectors, so the published product 
\begin_inset Formula $\times$
\end_inset

 industry matrix would have rank at most 
\begin_inset Formula $|m|$
\end_inset

. With four purposes and sixty-odd industries, that is far below the rank the published table actually has, and the accounting core could not reproduce it. Nor would the restriction simplify anything. Purchaser prices are industry-specific, so no economy-wide energy price emerges either way; the compact nesting already indexes the CES shares by industry, so the extra index costs no equations and no unknowns; and the interior still needs a prior. Industry-specific shares are what let the accounting core reproduce the published table exactly.
\end_layout

