\begin_layout Subsubsection
Household consumption types 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:cons-types"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Households are a single column 
\begin_inset Formula $C$
\end_inset

 in the use table. The household module first determines total consumption; a satellite then splits that total into types 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

 – housing energy, transport energy, food, and so on: 
\begin_inset Formula \[
D_{p,C}=\sum_{c}\mu_{p,c}C_{c}
\]
\end_inset

The information position here is better than for industries, and the difference matters. Eurostat's household consumption by purpose (
\family typewriter
nama_10_co3_p3
\family default
) is coarser than GREU's twelve groups: at the published three-digit COICOP depth only three are uniquely identified – car purchases, housing energy, and food and beverages. Two separate collapses do the damage: 
\family typewriter
CP011
\family default
 folds four food groups into one, and services tie non-food, housing and car fuels together, so none of those can be recovered without four-digit COICOP.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The two energy types are not taken from COICOP. Housing energy could be – 
\family typewriter
CP045
\family default
 identifies it – but car energy could not, because 
\family typewriter
CP072
\family default
 mixes motor fuels with spare parts and maintenance that belong to services. Both are instead filled from the physical energy accounts, which publish household heating, transport and other end use directly and reproduce the Danish household total to within 0.04 per cent. The division between heating and appliances differs from GREU's, but both fall inside housing energy and net to 
\begin_inset Formula $-0.26$
\end_inset


\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset

PJ; household transport differs by 
\begin_inset Formula $+0.36$
\end_inset


\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset

PJ. The tourism type has no COICOP row at all and is set from FIGARO's residents' expenditure abroad, which it matches exactly. All 27 member states publish 2020 at three-digit COICOP.
\end_layout

