\begin_layout Subsubsection
Capital types 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:capital-types"

\end_inset

 In the use table, fixed investment is a single column 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

. Buildings, vehicles and other machinery are not energy purposes; they are types of capital 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

, living in the production module or in a satellite attached to it: 
\begin_inset Formula \[
D_{K}=\sum_{k}I_k,\qquad I_{k}=\sum_{i}I_{k,i},\qquad I_{p,k}=\mu_{p,k}I_{k}
\]
\end_inset

The load-bearing object is 
\begin_inset Formula $I_{k,i}$
\end_inset

, the amount invested by type and by 
\shape italic
investing
\shape default
 industry. GREU does not use, and does not need, a matrix recording which industry produced the capital good for which investor: the model closes the capital market on 
\begin_inset Formula $I_{k,i}$
\end_inset

 alone and redistributes the resulting demand across supplying industries through the ordinary input-output machinery.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The use-side control is Eurostat gross fixed capital formation by asset and industry (
\family typewriter
nama_10_a64_p5
\family default
, transaction P51G, classification 
\family typewriter
asset10
\family default
). Buildings map to 
\family typewriter
N11KG
\family default
, transport equipment to 
\family typewriter
N1131G
\family default
, and other machinery is the remainder. For Denmark in 2020 these three national totals match the Danish investment file exactly. Thirteen member states publish the breakdown at NACE A64; fourteen publish only at A21. The product mix of the investment column itself still comes from FIGARO's P51G column, which is a concordance problem rather than an estimation problem.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
What remains to be constructed is the industry-by-asset interior inside A21 groups – together with the three GREU industries (
\family typewriter
55560
\family default
, 
\family typewriter
71000
\family default
, 
\family typewriter
off
\family default
) that cut across several A21 sections, so that the groups are not a partition. The cells are to be filled from a prior by cross-entropy minimisation, subject to known industry totals and the known group-by-asset aggregates; RAS is the one-year special case of that estimator. Whether identification holds is a rank check per A21 group, not only a count of industries per group. Two method choices are still open: whether Denmark is an acceptable donor, and whether shares may be held constant over time or need a smoothness penalty across years.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Two cautions carry over from the Danish data: zeros in the prior stick, so a Denmark-specific zero must be distinguished from a structural one, and Denmark is a weak donor for vehicles, because leasing and registration tax 
\shape italic
park
\shape default
 them in the renting industry rather than the using one.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Which prior belongs to which table
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Section
\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:purpose"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:capital-types"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 are easy to confuse, because both fill an unpublished interior from a Danish prior subject to known totals. They are not the same object and their priors are not interchangeable.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quote
The purpose split asks what a purchase was 
\shape italic
for
\shape default
. The capital split asks what 
\shape italic
kind of asset
\shape default
 was bought. A farm buys 100 if diesel: the purpose split asks whether it dried grain or ran the tractor. A farm buys a barn and a tractor: the capital split asks which was which.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A heating-versus-process prior must never be used to fill 
\begin_inset Formula $I_{k,i}$
\end_inset

, and a capital-type prior must never be used to fill Table
\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:stylised-purpose-table"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Physical energy markets
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:energy-markets"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $e\in\mathcal{E}\subset p$
\end_inset

 denote an energy product. For a homogeneous energy product, use the origin choice above. Replace 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\mu}_{e,u,o}$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{e,u}\hat{\mu}_{e,u,o}$
\end_inset

 and let physical market clearing determine 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{e,u}$
\end_inset

 for each use. CET or an energy-technology module allocates domestic output across industries through 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{e,i}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
National-account and physical units
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The national-account quantities 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{e,u,o}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $Y_{e,i}$
\end_inset

 do not measure physical energy. Map them to PJ:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
Q^{D}_{e,u,o}=\kappa^{D}_{e,u,o}D_{e,u,o}
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
Q^{D}_{e,u}=\kappa^{D}_{e,u}D_{e,u}
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
Q^{Y}_{e,i}=\kappa^{Y}_{e,i}Y_{e,i}
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Keep 
\begin_inset Formula $\kappa$
\end_inset

 fixed unless technology changes physical unit content. Budgets still use price times national-account quantity.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Total physical demand is
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
Q^{D}_{e}=\sum_{u}Q^{D}_{e,u}
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Quantity-preserving origin demand
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For energy, the physical balance for each use is
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
\sum_{o}\kappa^{D}_{e,u,o}D_{e,u,o}=\kappa^{D}_{e,u}D_{e,u}=Q^{D}_{e,u}
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It determines 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{e,u}$
\end_inset

 in the origin-demand equation above. With fixed 
\begin_inset Formula $\kappa$
\end_inset

, using prices per PJ only rescales the calibrated CES weights.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Source balance
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Supply by origin is
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
Q^{S}_{e,o}=\sum_{u}Q^{D}_{e,u,o}
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Thus, the aggregate physical balance is
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
\sum_{o}Q^{S}_{e,o}=Q^{D}_{e}
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Domestic supply is
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
Q^{S}_{e,\text{domestic}}=\sum_{i}Q^{Y}_{e,i}
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A energy-technology module can endogenize the domestic industry mix, but needs to ensure that the supply mix does not break the physical balance. A network module can specify losses that need to be added to the balances above.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Use-price differences per PJ come from 
\begin_inset Formula $\kappa^{D}_{e,u,o}$
\end_inset

, taxes, margin services, and VAT.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
An energy tax 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau^{E}_{e,u,o}$
\end_inset

 per PJ maps to 
\begin_inset Formula $t^{E}_{e,u,o}=\tau^{E}_{e,u,o}\kappa^{D}_{e,u,o}$
\end_inset

. Emissions use physical fuel at combustion or conversion.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Production (cost minimization)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:production"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Given the output to be produced, the production module calculates a marginal cost of production and the demand for each input factor into production.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Production function
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We assume a nested CES production function with
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
\text{factors of production}=k\cup r\cup l
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 are types of capital, 
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset

 are types of intermediate inputs, and 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

 are types of labor (with one type being perfectly fine).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
f=\text{factors of production}\cup\text{nests}
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Demand for the factor or nest 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{f}$
\end_inset

 in a specific industry (we omit the 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 index here) is
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
X_{f}=\mu_{f}X_{N}\left(\frac{P_{f}}{P_{N}}\right)^{-E_{N}}\forall f\in N
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where N is a production nest and 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{f}$
\end_inset

 is the usercost of the production factor or nest.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The usercost of a nest is given by the budget constraint
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
P_{N}X_{N}=\sum_{f\in N}P_{f}X_{f}
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Usercost of factors of production can either be simple spot prices, or come from complicated usercost expressions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We implement the nesting structure very compactly, using a single variable and demand equation for all factors of production, letting a mapping handle the nesting structure. The main upside is that this enables us to vary the nesting structure depending on the industry.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Capital adjustment costs and other frictions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:frictions"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Various frictions should be added to the simple usercost expressions, e.g.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
E.g. the usercost of capital is something like
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
P^{f}_{k}=P^{I}_{k}+\frac{\partial T^{\text{Corp}}}{\partial K_{k}}-\frac{1-\delta_{k,t+1}}{1+r_{t+1}}\left(P^{I}_{k,t+1}-\frac{\partial T^{\text{Corp}}_{t+1}}{\partial K_{k}}\right)+\text{marginal capital adjustment cost}
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The friction itself should be detailed in a satellite model, not in the production module. Friction variables are hooks that default to zero when no satellite model provides them.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Similarly, marginal taxes (here 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\partial T^{\text{Corp}}}{\partial K_{k}}$
\end_inset

) are defined elsewhere.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Output and marginal cost
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Output is the top nest less friction losses
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
Y=X_{N=\text{TopNest}}-\text{friction losses}
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Friction losses can for example be foregone output due to capital adjustment costs.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The marginal cost of production is
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
MC\cdot Y=P_{N=\text{TopNest}}Y+\text{production taxes}
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Frictions do not enter this equation directly. They affect the price through the usercosts inside the nest: the marginal capital adjustment cost enters the usercost of capital, and therefore 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{N=\text{TopNest}}$
\end_inset

, while the average (total) capital adjustment cost is subtracted from output at the top. 
\begin_inset Formula $MC$
\end_inset

 is therefore the cost of one more unit of output, and 
\begin_inset Formula $MC\cdot Y$
\end_inset

 is not total cost.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Behavior vs accounting
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Usercosts are behavior, not accounting. The usercost of capital contains the hurdle rate of the firm. Marginal cost and the markup derive from this subjective usercost, so they are model constructs, not neutral accounting. The sector account of firms records actual payments instead: revenue 
\begin_inset Formula $P^{Y}_{i}Y_{i}$
\end_inset

 less labor compensation, intermediate inputs, investments, and total (not marginal) taxes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Multiple types of capital
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the input-output system capital investments enter as a single column. In the production module, or in a satellite module, we split capital investments into multiple types of fixed assets, 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
D_{K}=\sum_{k}I_{k}
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
I_{k}=\sum_{i}I_{k,i}
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Endogenize 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{p,K}$
\end_inset

 by
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
D_{p,K}=\sum_{k}I_{p,k}
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For each type of capital, we assume fixed product shares.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
I_{p,k}=\mu_{p,k}I_{k}
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula $I_{k,i}$
\end_inset

 is given by cost-minimizing a production function subject to capital accumulation laws of motion.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Multiple types of intermediate inputs (energy)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We can split use in production into different types of intermediate inputs
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
D_{p,i}=\sum_{m}\mu_{p,m,i}M_{m,i}
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where for example
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
m=\left\{ \text{transport energy},\text{heating},\text{processing energy},\text{other intermediates}\right\} 
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
and 
\begin_inset Formula $M_{m,i}$
\end_inset

 is given by cost-minimizing a production function.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note that assuming identical product shares for energy inputs across industries is not needed and does not simplify much.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Pricing
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:pricing"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Markups in basic prices
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We use one fixed markup for each industry:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
P^{Y}_{p,i}=\theta_{i}\text{MC}_{p,i}
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The markup is the same for all products from industry 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

. Under the default product split, 
\begin_inset Formula $MC_{p,i}=MC_{i}$
\end_inset

. The CET module can give product-specific marginal costs.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Basic-price values sum by product and industry:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
P^{Y}_{p}Y_{p}=\sum_{i}P^{Y}_{p,i}Y_{p,i}
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
P^{Y}_{i}Y_{i}=\sum_{p}P^{Y}_{p,i}Y_{p,i}
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Base-year prices equal one, so measured volumes equal values. Product-by-use cells therefore do not identify price gaps or markup levels.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Trade and transport margins
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:margins"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Trade and transport margins are the wholesale, retail, and transport services needed to deliver a product to its user.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{S}=\left\{ \text{wholesale},\text{retail},\text{transport}\right\} \subset p$
\end_inset

 index margin service products. Each user has a fixed-share margin-service bundle. We use the standard origin shares for each margin service:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
P^{S}_{u}=\sum_{p\in\mathcal{S},o}\mu^{S}_{p,u}\mu_{p,u,o}P_{p,u,o}
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{p,u}$
\end_inset

 be purchaser use. Each unit needs 
\begin_inset Formula $a^{S}_{p,u}$
\end_inset

 units of the margin service bundle:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
S_{u}=\sum_{p}a^{S}_{p,u}D_{p,u}
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
S_{p,u,o}=\mu^{S}_{p,u}\mu_{p,u,o}S_{u}
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
S_{p,u,o}=0\quad\forall p\notin \mathcal{S}
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{p,u,o}$
\end_inset

 be the domestic basic price or import border price, with 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{p,u,\text{domestic}}=P^{Y}_{p}$
\end_inset

. The purchaser-use price is
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
P^{D}_{p,u,o}=\left(P_{p,u,o}+t_{p,u,o}+a^{S}_{p,u}P^{S}_{u}\right)\left(1+t^{\text{VAT}}_{p,u,o}\right)
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This is the price that enters into any substitution decision and the purchaser-use aggregate:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
P^{D}_{u}D_{u}=\sum_{p,o}P^{D}_{p,u,o}D_{p,u,o}.
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The product balance is
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
Y_{p,u}=D_{p,u,\text{domestic}}+S_{p,u,\text{domestic}}.
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Margin demand enters the product balance but not 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{u}$
\end_inset

. Its value is already in the purchaser price of direct demand, so do not add 
\begin_inset Formula $P^{S}_{u}S_{u}$
\end_inset

 to the user budget.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Households
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:households"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The household module determines total demand for private consumption (consumption savings decision).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Consumption savings decision
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Our default has one forward-looking household. It chooses total consumption 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{t}$
\end_inset

 and end-of-period real wealth 
\begin_inset Formula $W_{t}$
\end_inset

. A fixed share of current real disposable income follows a hand-to-mouth rule:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
C^{\mathrm{HtM}}_{t}=\lambda_{\mathrm{HtM}}Y^{d}_{t},\qquad0\leq\lambda_{\mathrm{HtM}}\leq1.
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{\mathrm{HtM}}$
\end_inset

 is a share of income, not a share of households. The household takes the external habit 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{t}=h\bar{C}_{t-1}$
\end_inset

 as given. Define consumption in excess of the reference level as
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
C^{\mathrm{xRef}}_{t}=C_{t}-H_{t}-C^{\mathrm{HtM}}_{t}.
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The household solves
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
\max_{\{C_{s},W_{s}\}^{\infty}_{s=t}}\sum^{\infty}_{s=t}\beta^{s-t}\left[\frac{(C^{\mathrm{xRef}}_{s})^{1-\sigma}}{1-\sigma}+\omega\frac{W^{1-\nu}_{s}}{1-\nu}\right].
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Use the simple budget constraint
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
W_{t}=(1+r_{t})W_{t-1}+Y^{d}_{t}-C_{t},
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{t}$
\end_inset

 is the real interest rate. The first-order condition for saving gives the Euler equation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
(C^{\mathrm{xRef}}_{t})^{-\sigma}=\omega W^{-\nu}_{t}+\beta(1+r_{t+1})(C^{\mathrm{xRef}}_{t+1})^{-\sigma}.
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Private consumption types
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We split private consumption into types 
\begin_inset Formula $c\in\mathcal{C}$
\end_inset

. The same product can enter more than one type, such as electricity for heating and transport. For each type, the default has fixed product shares:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
D_{p,C}=\sum_{c\in\mathcal{C}}\mu_{p,c}C_{c}
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A type-specific module can replace the fixed product shares with technology choices.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Consumption types are the leaves of a CES tree. Let
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
a=\mathcal{C}\cup\text{consumption nests}.
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
C_{a}=\mu_{a}C_{N}\left(\frac{P_{a}}{P_{N}}\right)^{-E_{N}}\quad\forall a\in N,
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
P_{N}C_{N}=\sum_{a\in N}P_{a}C_{a}.
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
At the root,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
C_{N=\mathrm{TopNest}}=C.
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Exports
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:exports"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We model export demand directly for domestic production of each product, 
\begin_inset Formula $Y_{p,X}$
\end_inset

, using an effective relative price 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{p,t}$
\end_inset

. This relative price equals the purchaser-to-foreign price ratio when the rigidity hook is zero.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Imports for re-export, 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{p,X,\text{import}}$
\end_inset

, are completely exogenous. To accommodate this, the import share of exports, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{p,X,o}$
\end_inset

, is endogenized.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Price rigidity
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Armington demand uses the effective relative price 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{p,t}$
\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
D_{p,X,\mathrm{domestic},t}R_{p,t}^{E_{p}^{X}}=X_{p,t}^{\mathrm{Market}},\qquad R_{p,t}=\frac{P_{p,X,\mathrm{domestic},t}^{D}+J_{p,t}}{P_{p,t}^{F}}.
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The hook 
\begin_inset Formula $J_{p,t}$
\end_inset

 is zero without the rigidity module. With the module, a cost based on squared changes in the effective price gives
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
\frac{J_{p,t}}{P_{p,t}^{F}}=-\psi^{X}\left(R_{p,t}-R_{p,t-1}\right)+\psi^{X}\beta^{X}\left(R_{p,t+1}-R_{p,t}\right).
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This lowers the short-run price response while keeping 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{p}^{X}$
\end_inset

 as the long-run elasticity. The future term makes the impact response depend on the duration of the shock. Setting 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta^{X}=0$
\end_inset

 gives the backward-looking rule. The initial state comes from calibration or the saved baseline, so a new start year is not itself a shock.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Government
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:government"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Sector accounting.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Including basic demand for public consumption and government transfers to other sectors.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There is no fiscal closure by default: government debt drifts.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Taxes
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:taxes"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Tax accounting and marginal tax rates (derivatives of total revenues wrt. to various tax bases).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Ideally, other modules should only use aggregate marginal tax rates and aggregate tax revenues. E.g., a household budget contains a total taxes paid variable, while a labor market decision equation uses only a marginal tax on labor income. The break down of marginal taxes into income brackets etc. is kept in the taxes module.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Labor market
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:labor"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The default model uses a forward-looking Phillips curve to set household employment relative to structural labor supply. Rest-of-world employment stays exogenous.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Firms
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:firms"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Sector accounting and endogenous revaluation rate of equity through a discounted cash flow valuation of the firm.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
One module covers both financial and non-financial corporations. The non-financial sector sums profits over industries; the sector accounting system redistributes them to owners.
\end_layout

